Washington, D.C. – U.S. Representative Colleen Hanabusa (HI-01), a member of the House Armed Services Committee, released the following statement in response to U.S. Secretary of Defense Chuck Hagel’s press conference previewing the Department of Defense's budget request for fiscal year 2015:
“I am very concerned with the budget provisions announced by Secretary Hagel today, which again underline the need to fully repeal the harmful cuts put in place by sequestration. One of the reasons I voted against the budget deal back in January was because it left intact more than $75 billion in defense cuts, while cutting billions more to other important domestic programs.  I have consistently advocated against arbitrary cuts to programs such as the Basic Allowance for Housing and commissaries, knowing that they will disproportionately affect people in Hawaii more than those in other states. 
“While the budget will still place priority on the Asia Pacific Region and Hawaii, the sheer magnitude of the endstrength reductions and other tough choices announced today come at the cost of military personnel and readiness.  I look forward to reviewing the budget proposal in its entirety, and I hope to work with my colleagues on the House Armed Services Committee to remedy harmful cuts to our national defense once and for all. ”
Several arbitrary cuts that Secretary Hagel announced today in his budget preview include:

Further reductions in troop strength and force structure in every military service – active and reserve;
A round of BRAC in 2017;
Half of the Navy’s cruiser fleet – or eleven ships – will be “laid up” and placed in reduced operating status while they are modernized;
The Marines will draw down to 182,000;
Further reduce active-duty Army end-strength to a range of 440-450,000 soldiers;
By 2017, endstrength reductions to 335,000 soldiers in the Army National Guard and 195,000 in the Reserves. If full sequestration stays in place, the Army National Guard would continue drawing down further, to 315,000. Army Reserves would draw down to 185,000;
Transfer of Army Guard Apache attack helicopters to active-duty units.
Slow the growth of tax-free housing allowances;
Reduce by $1 billion the annual direct subsidy provided to military commissaries, which now totals $1.4 billion;
Consolidating plans and adjusting deductibles and co-pays in the TRICARE program.

